Title = {The impact of place-based local contexts, presence, and narrative design
   on headset virtual reality engagement and learning in non-formal
   education settings},
Journal = {EDUCATION AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGIES},
Year = {2026},
Month = {2026 JUL 29},
Abstract = {While virtual reality (VR) learning experiences have been widely studied
   in formal educational settings, fewer studies have explored their impact
   in non-formal environments across diverse age groups. This study used
   path analysis to examine how place-based local context, immersion,
   presence, and VR design features (narrative, guidance and feedback)
   predict participants' engagement, industrial history content knowledge,
   affective learning, and perceptions of local environmental learning.
   Multivariate analysis of variance (MANOVA) was also conducted to explore
   differences by age, gender, race, and watershed residence. Results
   indicated a statistically significant increase in content knowledge from
   pretest to posttest, with no significant group differences for gender,
   race, or residence across outcome variables. Path analysis findings
   highlighted the importance of presence and local context for engagement
   and affective learning. Engagement was positively related to all
   learning outcomes and served as a significant mediator between local
   context and learning of the local environment, between local context and
   affective learning, and between presence and affective learning. VR
   design features, including the narrative component, did not
   significantly predict engagement or learning outcomes. The findings
   underscore the importance of authentic place-based contexts and presence
   in fostering engagement and learning in non-formal VR environments.
   While narrative design may contribute to VR learning experiences, its
   effects may be context-dependent, with experiential factors such as
   presence and local context playing a more central role in shaping
   engagement and learning outcomes.},
