Journal = {JOURNAL OF URBAN MANAGEMENT},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {15},
Number = {1},
Pages = {284-296},
Month = {MAR},
Abstract = {Urban expansion is pivotal in global urbanization, influencing human
   development and environmental sustainability. However, understanding
   urban growth dynamics, particularly the interaction between macro-level
   urban form and micro-level land use change, remains incomplete. This
   study analyzes Minsk, Belarus' urban expansion (1990-2020) to bridge
   this gap. We examine how macro-scale urban form and micro-scale land-use
   dynamics jointly shape urban growth. Using multi-temporal Landsat
   imagery, land use classification, landscape metrics (e.g., Proximity
   Expansion Index), and gradient analysis, we quantified Minsk's urban
   development patterns. Results show Minsk's expansion integrates
   dispersed peripheral growth with internal infill development. At the
   macro level, Minsk maintained a monocentric structure with decreasing
   urban land density outwards, while built-up land increased by 93.3 \%
   (198.08 km 2 ) during 1990-2020. Urban compactness also improved. At the
   micro level, edge-expansion patches dominated in number and area, with
   increasingly complex and fragmented forms, while infilling patches grew
   by 87.2 \%, enhancing spatial connectivity. These findings demonstrate
   that Minsk's urban expansion integrates outward sprawl with internal
   spatial optimization. By integrating macro and micro perspectives, this
   study proposes a comprehensive analytical framework for sustainable
   urban growth management. The insights are valuable for urban planners
   and policymakers in Minsk and comparable cities.},
